<article class="env-lemma" id="070Q">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/070Q">Lemma <span data-tag="070Q">21.24.1</span>.</a> In the situation described above. Denote $\mathcal{H}^ m = H^ m(\mathcal{F}^\bullet )$ the $m$th cohomology sheaf. Let $\mathcal{B} \subset \mathop{\mathrm{Ob}}\nolimits (\mathcal{C})$ be a subset. Let $d \in \mathbf{N}$. Assume </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
every object of $\mathcal{C}$ has a covering whose members are elements of $\mathcal{B}$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
for every $U \in \mathcal{B}$ we have $H^ p(U, \mathcal{H}^ q) = 0$ for $p > d$ and $q < 0$<a class="footnotemark" href="#a0000519717" id="a0000519717-mark"><sup>1</sup></a>. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Then (<a href="/tag/070P" data-tag="070P">21.24.0.1</a>) is a quasi-isomorphism. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By Derived Categories, Lemma <a href="/tag/070M" data-tag="070M">13.34.6</a> it suffices to show that the map $\mathcal{F}^\bullet \to R\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits \tau _{\geq -n} \mathcal{F}^\bullet $ is an isomorphism. This follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/0D6P" data-tag="0D6P">21.23.10</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>